These were the files used for the Vesta Cases, testing a spoiler location at the bottom of the left side, the mid of the left side, and the top of the left side. This was in order to determine where the maximmum velocity will happen on the roof to inform placement for the pilot.
The files within each folder correspond to the correct spoiler, and have been additionally modified so that the probes and region refinements are also in the correct location across all three cases. These have to be modified manually based on the exact locations of the spoiler on the building, so if you are changing one of the spoiler files or region refinements, be wary of what you are doing and where the spoiler actually is in the structure. Using paraView visualizations or having the OnShape view of the structure open can be helpful here.
Regarding the geometry formation and the OnShape, the structure of the Vesta Building is created in OpenFOAM through the blockMesh file. It has adjustable dimensions and peak height, so it can be used for future cases that are similar in nature to the Vesta, or to simply get a better understanding of what a crossflow direction slanted roof does to flow. 
For modifying the location of the spoiler, one can go to the Accelerate Wind Onshape in the Ike > Vesta folder. Use the Vesta_Simple file along with the Vesta Spoiler file to create the assembly that you want. The Vesta_Simple file will emulate how the blockMesh file forms the Vesta building but does not actually get used within the snappyHexMesh code. To export the spoiler in the correct location, a litle finagling is required, where you need to export the Vesta_Simple and Spoiler files as one part (on Fine resolution). Then, in WSL, run the surfaceCheck function on the file. It will decompose the file into 2 parts, a spoiler part and a building part. You can get rid of the building part as it is formed in the blockMesh, but save the spoiler part as spoiler.obj and include it in your constant/triSurface folder. This will mean the spoiler is in the correct location and orientation in the actual simulation. There might be an easier way to do this, but always verify that the spoiler was exported in the correct spot.
In blockMesh, the upstream/height/width buffers are all somewhat large. This is intentional and was found to be ideal through a sensivity study of the Vesta Building. These might be able to be refined, but can't be reduced too much. More information is located in the Coda page under CFD > Vesta Modeling > Dimension Sensitivity
The case itself is large, and was pushing close to 30mil cells for each of these cases. In this, make sure to update the amount of nodes that you use in the Run.slurm file to meet the 1 node per ~10mil cells rule and adjust time accordingly. 

IMPORTANT TO NOTE:
The layer formation in this code is god awful. If you are using this code for a more exact study, make sure to run a couple of debug cases to try to improve the cell formation. This likely isn't a problem with the snappyHexMesh, but more a problem with cell refinement. The density of cells should likely be upped both in the blockMesh dimensioning (x1.25 nCells in each dimension and increasing the refinementRegions in snappyHexMesh would be a good start) but beware that it might just be an unsolvable problem too. SnappyHexMesh doesn't like very shallow slants because it tries to make them into perpendicular surfaces and a lot of different settings have been experimented with in snappyHexMesh already to no avail.
